<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  accessibilityEventTypes is deliberately narrow. typeWindowStateChanged fires when the user opens
  a screen or switches app — a handful of times a minute at worst. typeWindowContentChanged, which
  is what a service would need to follow scrolling, fires dozens of times a second in any feed and
  is the classic way an accessibility service ruins a phone's battery. ArcX takes the staleness
  instead; see ArcxAccessibilityService for what that costs.

  notificationTimeout throttles the events the system does deliver, so a burst of window changes
  during an app launch collapses into one callback.

  flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows is what makes getWindows() return anything, which is how the
  service tells the user's app apart from ArcX's own window and from system UI. Note that it does
  not widen this as far as you would hope: Android exposes only the top task's application window,
  so a window sitting behind ArcX's runner is unreadable no matter what is set here.

  canTakeScreenshot is what vision workflows run on, and it is the reason ArcX does not need
  MediaProjection: no per-session system consent dialog, so a workflow that looks at the screen is
  still one tap. The attribute arrived in API 30 and older platforms ignore it, which is fine —
  ArcxAccessibilityService gates the call on the capability actually being granted rather than on
  having asked for it.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault|flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:canTakeScreenshot="true"
    android:description="@string/arcx_accessibility_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="200" />
